>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `CLARIFY_TAC`

``` hol4
classicalLib.CLARIFY_TAC : thm list -> tactic
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Repeatedly applies non-branching safe classical-reasoning steps.

`CLARIFY_TAC thms` uses the current theory's classical rule set. Its base
cascade performs built-in safe steps, safe rules with at most one premise,
and two-premise safe rules when one branch closes immediately by assumption
or contradiction. Rules with three or more premises and genuinely branching
two-premise rules are not clarification steps. Unlike the full safe cascade,
it does not perform assumption modus ponens. Registered safe wrappers also
participate. In particular, clarification can simplify an implication while
leaving an unforced conjunction conclusion intact.

The theorem-list vocabulary is the same as for `SAFE_TAC`: `SIntro`,
`Intro`, `SElim`, `Elim`, `SDest`, `Dest` and `Del` modify a temporary rule
set. Plain theorems default to unsafe introduction rules and are not used by
this safe-only tactic. Generic simplifier markers are ignored as classical
rules. The tactic uses matching and does not instantiate goal variables.

The tactic need not solve the goal; it can succeed with residual goals.

### Failure

After its temporary rule set has been constructed, fails exactly when
repeated clarification leaves the goal unchanged, up to alpha conversion.
Use `TRY (CLARIFY_TAC thms)` to obtain no-op success.
Malformed extra rules can also cause failure while the theorem list is
processed.

### Example

Here clarification introduces the antecedent and then closes by assumption:

``` hol4
Theorem clarify_tac_example:
  p ==> p
Proof
  classicalLib.CLARIFY_TAC []
QED
```

By contrast, on the goal `p /\ q` with neither conjunct available,
`CLARIFY_TAC []` fails rather than creating two subgoals; `SAFE_TAC []`
performs that split.

